1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,667
This video shows doeff running one battle a hundred times, one task per fight.

2
00:00:05,667 --> 00:00:08,300
doeff is algebraic effects for Python.

3
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:16,450
We look at win_rate, and at scheduled, the handler that runs the tasks. We follow Spawn and Gather.

4
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:24,850
win_rate is a program too. It yields Spawn once per seed, and each Spawn starts a battle as a task.

5
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:35,950
Each task carries its own stack, so the hundred fights do not share a random generator.

6
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:45,317
The tasks take turns on one thread. At each Spawn, scheduled keeps the k of win_rate and runs the new task.

7
00:00:45,317 --> 00:00:52,000
No effect of a fight reaches scheduled, so the task runs to its end, and then win_rate goes on.

8
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Gather waits for every task and gives back their results. Here, every fight has already ended.

9
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:14,050
On the same hundred seeds, the random bot wins seventy-three fights.

10
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:21,200
Change one argument, the player, and the Monte Carlo bot wins all one hundred.

11
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:32,983
A task's own handlers do not take Played, so it travels out past the Spawn,

12
00:01:32,983 --> 00:01:35,850
and one handler hears all hundred fights.
